{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"vector","title":"std::vector","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":49,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A dynamic array: contiguous storage that grows automatically. The container you should reach for first.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Core operations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v;                      // empty\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; zeros(5);               // 5 × 0\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; sevens(3, 7);           // 3 × 7\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; list{1, 2, 3};          // initializer list\n\n    v.push_back(10);                         // append\n    v.emplace_back(20);                      // construct in place\n    v.insert(v.begin(), 5);                  // insert at front: O(n)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.front() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.back() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v[1] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    v.pop_back();                            // remove last\n    v.erase(v.begin());                      // remove first: O(n)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v[0] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    list.resize(5);                          // grow with zeros\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; list[4] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; zeros.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sevens[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    list.clear();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; list.empty() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 5 20 10\n1 10\n0 5 7\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-maximize\"></i>Size vs capacity</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v;\n    std::size_t lastCap = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100; ++i) {\n        v.push_back(i);\n        if (v.capacity() != lastCap) { lastCap = v.capacity(); std::cout &lt;&lt; lastCap &lt;&lt; ' '; }\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; r;\n    r.reserve(100);                    // one allocation up front\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; r.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; r.capacity() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    v.resize(10);\n    v.shrink_to_fit();                 // non-binding request to release spare capacity\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.capacity() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128\n0 100\n10 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Growth</div><p>When full, <code>push_back</code> allocates a bigger buffer (libc++ and libstdc++ double it; MSVC grows by 1.5×), moves the elements over and frees the old one. Each element is moved O(1) times on average → <strong>amortised O(1)</strong> append. If you know the final size, <code>reserve</code> it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Iterator invalidation</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Invalidates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>push_back</code> / <code>emplace_back</code> / <code>insert</code> causing reallocation</td><td><strong>All</strong> iterators, pointers, references</td></tr><tr><td><code>insert</code> without reallocation</td><td>Those at or after the insertion point</td></tr><tr><td><code>erase</code></td><td>Those at or after the erased element</td></tr><tr><td><code>reserve</code>, <code>shrink_to_fit</code></td><td>All, if the capacity changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};\n    for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) {     // erase while iterating: use the returned iterator\n        if (*it % 2 == 0) it = v.erase(it);\n        else ++it;\n    }\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::erase_if(v, [](int x) { return x &gt; 3; });   // C++20 one-liner\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>135\n13</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>2-D vectors &amp; useful members</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; grid(3, std::vector&lt;int&gt;(4, 0));   // 3 rows × 4 cols\n    grid[1][2] = 5;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; grid.size() &lt;&lt; 'x' &lt;&lt; grid[0].size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; grid[1][2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; a{1, 2, 3}, b{1, 2, 4};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (a &lt; b) &lt;&lt; (a == b) &lt;&lt; '\\n';        // lexicographic comparison\n    a.swap(b);                                       // O(1)\n    a.assign(2, 9);                                  // replace contents\n    int* raw = a.data();                             // pointer to the contiguous buffer\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; raw[1] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.back() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3x4 5\n10\n2 9 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>v[i]</code> doesn't check bounds (out-of-range is UB); <code>v.at(i)</code> throws <code>std::out_of_range</code>. And <code>std::vector&lt;bool&gt;</code> is a space-optimised bit-packed specialisation: <code>&amp;v[0]</code> isn't a <code>bool*</code> and its elements aren't real references.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens when you push_back into a full vector?</summary><div><p>It allocates a larger block (typically 2× or 1.5× the capacity), move-constructs (or copies, if the move constructor isn't <code>noexcept</code>) the existing elements into it, constructs the new element, destroys the old elements and frees the old block. All iterators, pointers and references into the vector are invalidated.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between reserve and resize?</summary><div><p><code>reserve(n)</code> only changes <em>capacity</em> — it allocates room for <code>n</code> elements without creating any; <code>size()</code> is unchanged. <code>resize(n)</code> changes <em>size</em>: it creates value-initialised elements (or destroys extras), so <code>v[n-1]</code> becomes valid.</p></div></details></section>"}